s=\"ltx_ref\">25</a>]</cite> and a baseline based on <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib9\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">9</a>]</cite>. while <cite class=\"ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite\">[<a href=\"#bib.bib25\" title=\"\" class=\"ltx_ref\">25</a>]</cite> uses Pascal segmentation masks, ImageNet bounding boxes, and WordNet taxonomy, our model needs no explicit supervision. Nonetheless, it obtains comparable results.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n",
        "footnotes": [
            "[9]\n\nS. Divvala, A. Farhadi, and C. Guestrin.\n\n\nLearning everything about anything: Webly-supervised visual concept\nlearning.\n\n\nIn CVPR, 2014.",
            "[25]\n\nD. Kuettel, M. Guillaumin, and V. Ferrari.\n\n\nSegmentation propagation in ImageNet.\n\n\nIn ECCV, 2012."
        ],
        "references": "We follow the experimental set up of [25]222The ground-truth masks and the foreground masks produced by the segmentation propagation method of [25] are both available on their project website, enabling us to compare our results. and use a similar procedure as detailed earlier for segmenting the images. Table 2 compares our results with [25]. Our method obtains similar levels of precision with a small drop in Jaccard. Note that our method does not require any form of manual supervision, while [25] uses a (i) seed set of segmentation masks (VOC2012), (ii) a taxonomy structure (WordNet), and (iii) a held-out set of labeled images (with bounding boxes) to train their model parameters. Figure 2 shows some qualitative examples of comparisons between our method and [25]."
    },
    "id_table_3": {
